# Playbooks

Every playbook ends the same way: `pnpm fix`, then `pnpm check` and `pnpm test`, all green, then
commit.

## Add a config option

1. Write the failing tests first: validation in `src/config/index.test.ts`, output in the suite of
   the layer that owns it (`src/core/index.test.ts`, `src/generator/*.test.ts`).
2. Add the field to the `ConfigSchema` union in `src/config/index.ts` — to `sharedFields`, or to
   the one variant it belongs to — with a check whose message names what is expected, and to
   `WakuseiConfig` / `resolveLayout` in `src/core/layout.ts` if it affects paths.
3. Implement it in `core/` (the Effect program) and `generator/` / `helper/` (the pure builders),
   respecting the layering. Both entry points reach it through `orpc()`; never special-case one.
4. If the CLI should expose it, add the flag in `src/cli/index.ts` and pass it through
   `parseConfig`.
5. Document it in `packages/wakusei/README.md` (`Full Config Reference`, and `CLI` for a flag).

## Fix a generator bug

1. Reproduce it as a failing test at the smallest document that shows it, asserting the exact
   generated source.
2. Find the layer that owns the mistake — parsing and Components (oas-truth), a schema expression
   (schema-to-library), a code builder (`generator/`), composition (`helper/`), the program
   (`core/`), the merge (`merge/`) — and fix it there, not downstream. A mistake in oas-truth or
   schema-to-library is fixed upstream, never by string-patching its output here.
3. Check whether the other two schema libraries, the other mode and single-file output have the
   same mistake.

## A request to support a vendor extension (`x-*`)

1. Classify it with the architecture rule's three layers.
2. A or B: it belongs in schema-to-library (and oas-truth's `Schema` type), not here — wakusei
   only passes it through.
3. C (code injection): decline, citing the architecture rule. Do not add a flag, an option or an
   allowlist for it.

## Check a change end-to-end

For a change to the generated code, the merge, the CLI or the Vite plugin:

1. `vp run wakusei#build`.
2. `pnpm -F @wakusei/test test`: every case regenerated through `dist` (overlays merged), `tsc`
   per case against the real libraries, the routers over HTTP, and the Vite dev server.
3. Read the diff of `test/__generated__` for the cases the change touches.
4. For `--watch` or the Vite plugin, also run it by hand in a scratch directory: edit the spec and
   the config, and confirm one pass per save and that handler bodies survive.

## Add an end-to-end case

1. `test/cases/<name>/wakusei.config.ts` reading a document in `test/specs` and writing to
   `../../__generated__/<name>`.
2. A `tsconfig.json` extending `../../tsconfig.base.json` over `../../__generated__/<name>`,
   unless the case is only driven at runtime.
3. When it writes handler stubs that the runtime suite calls, an `overlay` link to
   `../../overlays/handlers`, with any new procedure's body added there.
4. A `runtime/` assertion when the case changes behavior over HTTP.

## Clear a lint failure

1. Run `pnpm check` and read which linter failed and the rule name in the message.
2. Try `pnpm fix` first — formatting, import order, markdownlint and textlint terms are
   autofixable. Read what textlint's `terminology` changed: a package name it "corrected" belongs
   in a code span instead.
3. Otherwise **change the code, not the rule.** The rules encode the design: an assertion ban means
   narrow with a guard; a `no-restricted-imports` message means the dependency direction is wrong;
   `prefer-each` means `it.each`.
4. An unknown word from cspell is one line in `project-words.txt` if it is a real project term, a
   file-local `cspell:ignore` if it is deliberate test data, and a rename or a US spelling if it
   is an abbreviation or a typo.
5. Only if an oxlint rule genuinely cannot hold, add
   `// oxlint-disable-next-line rule-name -- reason` on that line, and say in the report that you
   added a suppression and why. A secretlint finding is never suppressed.

## Add a linter rule

1. Check its category; correctness / suspicious / perf are already errors.
2. Add it to the `lint` section of `packages/wakusei/vite.config.ts` in its section, with the
   reason.
3. Run `pnpm check` and fix every finding in the code.

## Bump actionlint or zizmor

1. Pick the release and read its notes for new findings.
2. In `scripts/actionlint.ts` / `scripts/zizmor.ts`, change `VERSION` and every checksum together:
   actionlint's from the release's `*_checksums.txt`, zizmor's from each asset's `digest` in
   `https://api.github.com/repos/zizmorcore/zizmor/releases/tags/v<VERSION>`.
3. `pnpm lint`: the script downloads, verifies and runs the new binary. Fix new findings in the
   workflow, or excuse one with its reason next to it.

## Bump effect

1. Find the release candidate oas-truth's schema-to-library now uses; that is the target.
2. Change `effect` and `@effect/platform-node` in `packages/wakusei/package.json` (both exact)
   and the `@effect/platform-node-shared` override in `pnpm-workspace.yaml` together, then
   `pnpm install`.
3. `pnpm check`. The RC moves the Schema and CLI APIs, so expect errors in `config/` and `cli/`.
4. `pnpm test`, and read every behavior difference rather than adjusting an assertion to match.
5. Say in the commit body which upstream changes forced code changes.

## Bump TypeSpec

1. Change every `@typespec/*` entry in the `catalog` of `pnpm-workspace.yaml` to the new release
   (today `@typespec/http`), never a `package.json`. oas-truth's TypeSpec peers
   (`@typespec/compiler`, `@typespec/openapi3`) follow that release through the lockfile.
2. `pnpm install`, then `node scripts/typespec.ts` (also part of `pnpm lint`): it fails until
   `pnpm-lock.yaml` holds one release, and prints the `pnpm update` that re-resolves the stragglers.
3. `pnpm test`: TypeSpec input is compiled for real, so read any changed expectation.

## Bump oas-truth

1. Change the version; if it is younger than pnpm's minimum release age, add it to
   `minimumReleaseAgeExclude` in `pnpm-workspace.yaml` (and drop entries that no longer need it).
2. `pnpm install`, `pnpm test`. A changed expectation is a changed generated output: read it and
   decide whether it is right before updating the test.
3. If oas-truth moved schema-to-library to a new Effect RC, do the effect bump in the same change.

## Release (only when asked)

1. Confirm `main` is green and `packages/wakusei/package.json` is not `"private": true` (the
   release workflow publishes nothing while it is).
2. Bump `version` in `packages/wakusei/package.json` only (SemVer; while the version is `0.x`, a
   breaking change to the config, the CLI or the generated output is a minor bump).
3. `pnpm check` and `pnpm test`, then commit `chore: release wakusei <version>` and push to
   `main`.
4. Watch Test, then `release`: it tags `v<version>`, publishes through `pnpm publish` and creates
   the GitHub release from that commit. Never publish or tag by hand.
5. If a job fails, fix the cause and re-run the failed jobs. To fix a published release, bump
   again — never reuse a version.
